window.__hvCxRegister("rep", {"v":1,"dialogue":"rep","title":"Republic","speechLabel":"On this book","eras":[{"id":"early-empire","label":"Early Roman Empire"},{"id":"late-antiquity","label":"Late antiquity"},{"id":"byzantine","label":"Byzantium"},{"id":"islamic","label":"The Islamic world"},{"id":"renaissance","label":"Renaissance"},{"id":"modern","label":"Modern"}],"items":[{"c":"Plutarch","e":"early-empire","y":100,"w":"Platonic Questions","k":"plut-moralia-4","p":1689,"ref":"Question IX","t":"Since Plato in his Commonwealth, discoursing of the faculties of the soul, has very well compared the symphony of reason and of …","x":"Did Plato Place the Rational or the Irascible Faculty in the Middle? For he is Not Clear in the Point.","tier":"passage","r":[[2209,2219]],"rs":"441e–443e"},{"c":"Plutarch","e":"early-empire","y":100,"w":"Platonic Questions","k":"plut-moralia-4","p":1648,"ref":"Question III","t":"In the Republic, he assumes the universe, as one line to be cut into two unequal parts; again he cuts each of these parts in two …","x":"But what Does he Mean by Dividing the Universe into Unequal Parts? And which of the Sections, the Intelligible or the Sensible, is the Greater? For in this he has Not Explained Himself.","tier":"passage","r":[[2548,2559]],"rs":"509d–511e"},{"c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"On the Republic (extract, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-1","p":98,"ref":"Book IV","t":"On the four virtues and the parts of the soul (Book IV)","x":"The following extract from the exposition of the more difficult questions in the Republic, by Proclus, (p. 407) will, I doubt not, from its great excellence be very acceptable to the reader. It is concerning the demonstrations in …","tier":"speech","r":[[2138,2229]],"rs":"427d–445e"},{"c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":9,"ref":"","t":"General Introduction to the Republic","x":"The design of Plato, says Proclus, in this dialogue, is both concerning a polity and true justice, not as two distinct things, but as the same with each other. For what justice is in one soul, that such a polity as is delineated …","tier":"dialogue","r":[[1635,3109]],"rs":"the whole dialogue"},{"c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Additional Notes on the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-notes-1","p":78,"ref":"Book I","t":"“Are they to have torches, and give them to one another?”","x":"In the Panathenæan, Hephæstian, and Promethean festivals, it was customary for young men to run with torches or lamps: and in this contest he alone was victorious, whose lamp remained unextinguished in the race. As a lamp or …","tier":"passage","r":[[1639,1641]],"rs":"328a"},{"c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Additional Notes on the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-notes-1","p":80,"ref":"Book I","t":"“This nocturnal solemnity was the lesser Panathenæa”","x":"As in the greater Panathenæa the veil of Minerva was carried about in which the Giants were represented vanquished by the Olympian Gods, so in the lesser Panathenæa another veil was exhibited, in which the Athenians, who were the …","tier":"passage","r":[[1639,1641]],"rs":"328a"},{"c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Additional Notes on the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-notes-1","p":82,"ref":"Book I","t":"“The Seriphian”","x":"Seriphus, one of the islands of the Cyclades, and a city in it; whence its inhabitants were called Seriphians. Schol. Græc. in Plat. p. 144.","tier":"passage","r":[[1648,1650]],"rs":"329e"},{"c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Additional Notes on the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-notes-1","p":84,"ref":"Book I","t":"“As Pindar says”","x":"These verses of Pindar are only to be found in the fragments ascribed to him.","tier":"passage","r":[[1653,1655]],"rs":"330e"},{"c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Additional Notes on the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-notes-1","p":86,"ref":"Book I","t":"“Thrasymachus”","x":"This Thrasymachus was a sophist, and is mentioned by Aristotle in the last book of his Sophistical Arguments. Nothing can more clearly show the Herculean strength of the reasoning which Socrates here employs, than that it was …","tier":"passage","r":[[1670,1672]],"rs":"334b"},{"c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":17,"ref":"","t":"Introduction to Books II–III, with Proclus' apology for the fables of Homer","x":"SECOND AND THIRD BOOKS OF THE REPUBLIC, CONTAINING AN APOLOGY FOR THE FABLES OF HOMER.","tier":"speech","r":[[1785,2086]],"rs":"357a–417b"},{"c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Additional Notes on the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-notes-1","p":89,"ref":"Book II","t":"“Is not God essentially good?”","x":"It is well observed by Proclus (in Plat. Polit. p. 355.), that when Plato says in this place, God is essentially good, he means every God; for the addition of the article either alone signifies transcendency, as when we say the …","tier":"passage","r":[[1895,1897]],"rs":"379b"},{"c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Additional Notes on the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-notes-1","p":92,"ref":"Book III","t":"“And we should send him to some other city, pouring oil on…”","x":"The Greek Scholium on this part is as follows: παροιμια επι των απο της χρειας αφιεμενων· και γαρ επι των ἑορτων εοικασιν ὁι ανθρωποι μυρῳ κατα της κεφαλης καταχεισθαι, ὡς αν σχολαζοντες απο των εργων, και μονον σχολαζοντες …","tier":"passage","r":[[1989,1991]],"rs":"398a"},{"c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Additional Notes on the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-notes-1","p":124,"ref":"Book V","t":"“The riddle of children about the Eunuch striking the bat”","x":"This, according to the Greek Scholiast on this part, was the riddle of Clearchus, and is as follows: A man and not a man, seeing and yet not seeing, struck and yet did not strike, a bird and not a bird, sitting and not sitting …","tier":"passage","r":[[2396,2398]],"rs":"479c"},{"c":"Al-Farabi","e":"islamic","y":942,"w":"The Virtuous City","k":"farabi-virtuous-city","p":243,"ref":"XXVI","t":"Why human beings need association","x":"Every human being is so constituted by nature that, for his subsistence and for reaching the most excellent of his perfections, he needs many things that he cannot provide all by himself alone; rather he needs people, each of …","tier":"passage","r":[[1846,1847]],"rs":"369b–369c"},{"c":"Al-Farabi","e":"islamic","y":942,"w":"The Virtuous City","k":"farabi-virtuous-city","p":248,"ref":"XXVI","t":"The virtuous city is like a healthy body","x":"The virtuous city resembles the complete and healthy body, all of whose organs assist one another to complete the life of the animal and to preserve it in it.","tier":"passage","r":[[2312,2314]],"rs":"462c–462e"},{"c":"Al-Farabi","e":"islamic","y":942,"w":"The Virtuous City","k":"farabi-virtuous-city","p":262,"ref":"XXVIIb","t":"The ruler must be one who has become intellect in act","x":"That human being must be a human being whom no human being rules at all. Such a human being is one who has been perfected and has become intellect and intelligible in act; whose imaginative faculty has been perfected by nature to …","tier":"passage","r":[[2367,2369]],"rs":"473c–473e"},{"c":"Al-Farabi","e":"islamic","y":942,"w":"The Virtuous City","k":"farabi-virtuous-city","p":269,"ref":"XXVIII","t":"The twelve natural qualities of the first ruler","x":"This, then, is the ruler over whom no other human being rules at all. He is the imam; he is the first ruler of the virtuous city, the ruler of the virtuous nation, and the ruler of the whole inhabited part of the earth. This …","tier":"passage","r":[[2425,2435]],"rs":"485a–487a"},{"c":"Al-Farabi","e":"islamic","y":942,"w":"The Virtuous City","k":"farabi-virtuous-city","p":283,"ref":"XXVIII","t":"Such a nature is found only rarely, one at a time","x":"The union of all these in a single human being is difficult; for that reason one who is born with this nature is found only one at a time, and he is among the rarest of men. If such a one is found in the virtuous city, and after …","tier":"passage","r":[[2516,2518]],"rs":"503b–503d"},{"c":"Al-Farabi","e":"islamic","y":942,"w":"The Virtuous City","k":"farabi-virtuous-city","p":211,"ref":"XXII","t":"The Active Intellect gives the intellect what the sun gives sight","x":"There is not in the substance of the visual faculty that is in the eye what suffices for it to become sight in act, nor in the substances of colours what suffices for them to become seen and visible in act. Rather, the sun gives …","tier":"passage","r":[[2536,2546]],"rs":"507b–509b"},{"c":"Al-Farabi","e":"islamic","y":942,"w":"The Virtuous City","k":"farabi-virtuous-city","p":376,"ref":"XXXV","t":"The ignorant cities' view: justice is conquest","x":"Justice, then, is mutual conquest, and justice is that whichever of them happens to prevail should subdue the other. The one subdued is either deprived, by being overcome, of the safety of his body, perishing and being destroyed, …","tier":"passage","r":[[1692,1695]],"rs":"338c–339a"},{"c":"Al-Farabi","e":"islamic","y":942,"w":"The Virtuous City","k":"farabi-virtuous-city","p":378,"ref":"XXXV","t":"Justice as a truce between those of equal strength","x":"That is, it is as if there were two individuals or two groups, each equal to the other in strength, who take turns in subduing each other. This goes on a long time between them; each suffers bitterly, and they come to a state …","tier":"passage","r":[[1794,1796]],"rs":"358e–359b"},{"c":"Al-Farabi","e":"islamic","y":942,"w":"The Virtuous City","k":"farabi-virtuous-city","p":396,"ref":"XXXVII","t":"The ignorant cities: necessity, wealth, baseness, honour, the collective city","x":"Among the ignorant cities are the necessary city, the city of exchange, the base city, the city of honour, and the collective city. In the others, apart from the collective city, the aim of the inhabitants is a single kind of …","tier":"speech","r":[[2715,2847]],"rs":"543a–569c"}],"rk":"rep","glosses":[{"s":1635,"ri":1,"ph":"to offer my prayers to the goddess","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":12,"ref":"","n":"Proclus, as Taylor reports, cites the opening, the descent to the Piraeus, the prayer and the festival, as examples of the narrative side of the dialogue's mixed form.","q":"and alone preserves an accurate narration of persons and things; such as are—descending to the Piræum, praying to the goddess, beholding the festival, and the like."},{"s":1640,"ri":4,"ph":"Will they carry torches and pass them to one another","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Additional Notes on the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-notes-1","p":79,"ref":"Book I","n":"Taylor explains that young men ran with torches or lamps in the Panathenaic, Hephaestian and Promethean festivals, and the victor was the one whose lamp stayed alight throughout the race.","q":"In the Panathenæan, Hephæstian, and Promethean festivals, it was customary for young men to run with torches or lamps: and in this contest he alone was victorious, whose lamp remained unextinguished in the race."},{"s":1640,"ri":4,"ph":"Will they carry torches and pass them to one another","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Additional Notes on the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-notes-1","p":79,"ref":"Book I","n":"Taylor reads the torch, whose fire naturally rises, as an image of the rational part, suggesting the custom signifies that the true victor in life is he whose reason never goes dormant.","q":"this custom perhaps was intended to signify that he is the true conqueror in the race of life, whose rational part is not extinguished, or, in other words, does not become dormant in the career."},{"s":1641,"ri":5,"ph":"an all-night festival","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Additional Notes on the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-notes-1","p":81,"ref":"Book I","n":"Taylor identifies the night festival as the lesser Panathenaea, at which a veil of Minerva was shown depicting the Athenians, her pupils, victorious over the inhabitants of the Atlantic island.","q":"so in the lesser Panathenæa another veil was exhibited, in which the Athenians, who were the pupils of Minerva, were represented victorious in the battle against the inhabitants of the Atlantic island."},{"s":1641,"ri":5,"ph":"watch the night festival","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Additional Notes on the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-notes-1","p":81,"ref":"Book I","n":"Taylor, following Proclus, takes the Panathenaic festivals to signify the order flowing into the world from intellect, the Athenian victory over the Atlantics showing the better coordination of things subduing the worse.","q":"These festivals signified the beautiful order which proceeds into the world from intellect, and the unconfused distinction of mundane contrarieties."},{"s":1642,"ri":6,"ph":"Thrasymachus the Chalcedonian","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Additional Notes on the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-notes-1","p":87,"ref":"Book I","n":"Taylor identifies Thrasymachus as a sophist, the same man whom Aristotle mentions in the last book of his Sophistical Arguments.","q":"This Thrasymachus was a sophist, and is mentioned by Aristotle in the last book of his Sophistical Arguments."},{"s":1650,"ri":14,"ph":"the man from Seriphos","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Additional Notes on the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-notes-1","p":83,"ref":"Book I","n":"Taylor, citing the Greek scholia, explains that Seriphus is one of the islands of the Cyclades, with a city on it, and that its inhabitants were called Seriphians.","q":"Seriphus, one of the islands of the Cyclades, and a city in it; whence its inhabitants were called Seriphians."},{"s":1654,"ri":18,"ph":"as Pindar says","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Additional Notes on the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-notes-1","p":85,"ref":"Book I","n":"Taylor notes that the verses of Pindar quoted here by Cephalus are to be found only among the fragments ascribed to the poet.","q":"These verses of Pindar are only to be found in the fragments ascribed to him."},{"s":1681,"ri":45,"ph":"Gathering himself up like a wild beast","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Additional Notes on the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-notes-1","p":87,"ref":"Book I","n":"Taylor calls Thrasymachus a savage sophist and takes Socrates' success in taming him as clear proof of the Herculean strength of his reasoning.","q":"Nothing can more clearly show the Herculean strength of the reasoning which Socrates here employs, than that it was able to tame this savage sophist."},{"s":1757,"ri":121,"ph":"a city or an army, robbers or thieves","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":13,"ref":"","n":"Proclus, as Taylor reports, develops the argument: a city, army or house wholly unjust falls into sedition and can effect nothing, so whatever power injustice has depends on some vestige of justice.","q":"Of necessity, therefore, every one who acts unjustly, if he is able to effect any thing whatever, must possess some vestige of justice; so inseparable is the union between power and justice."},{"s":1762,"ri":126,"ph":"total degenerates, who are completely unjust, are also completely incapable","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":15,"ref":"","n":"Proclus, as Taylor reports, draws from this Amelius' corollary: greater injustice often produces lesser evils, since perfect injustice makes life inefficacious, while justice mixed with injustice yields action.","q":"For, when injustice perfectly subdues the soul, life is inefficacious; but, when justice is associated with injustice, a certain action is the result."},{"s":1843,"ri":195,"ph":"read the smaller letters after we have first read the larger ones","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"On the Republic (extract, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-1","p":105,"ref":"Book IV","n":"Proclus explains the image of larger and smaller letters: virtue in one soul is more indivisible than in cities, and the virtues of a whole city are images of those in one soul.","q":"and says, that he is willing to behold, as it were, in larger, what is written in smaller letters: for virtue in the habitation of one soul is more impartible than in cities, and the virtues of a whole city are images of those in one soul"},{"s":1843,"ri":195,"ph":"the same letters are situated somewhere else, but larger","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":11,"ref":"","n":"Proclus, as Taylor reports, takes the image to show that justice and polity differ only in matter, like small and large letters, but share one form; the passage is from justice in one to justice in many.","q":"The matter therefore of justice and a polity is different, in the same manner as that of small and large letters, but the form is the same."},{"s":1889,"ri":241,"ph":"after sacrificing not some common piglet, but an enormous beast","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":28,"ref":"","n":"Proclus argues that requiring such fables to be heard in secret after a great sacrifice shows Socrates treats them as mystic, coordinated with the holiest initiations, not as worthless.","q":"For to assert that such fables ought to be used in secret with a sacrifice the greatest and most perfect , manifests that the contemplation of them is mystic, and that they elevate the souls of the hearers to sublime speculations."},{"s":1889,"ri":241,"ph":"Uranus performed the deeds that Hesiod says he performed","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":30,"ref":"","n":"Proclus reads the castration of Heaven as signifying the separation of the Titanic series from the connective order, the progression of secondary natures from causes that remain undiminished.","q":"The castrations of Heaven obscurely signify the separation of the Titanic series from the connective order."},{"s":1889,"ri":241,"ph":"what he suffered at the hands of his son","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":30,"ref":"","n":"Proclus interprets the bonds of Saturn as the union of the whole fabrication of the universe with Saturn's intellectual and paternal supremacy; a divine bond is contact with causes, not restraint.","q":"But the Saturnian bonds manifest the union of the whole fabrication of the universe, with the intellectual and paternal supremacy of Saturn."},{"s":1891,"ri":243,"ph":"the battles of gods and giants","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":39,"ref":"","n":"Proclus says fables oppose Titans to Bacchus and Giants to Jupiter because those powers divide and multiply demiurgic power, against the undivided wholeness of the prior artificers.","q":"On this account, as it appears to me, they assert that the Titans were the antagonists of Bacchus, and the Giants of Jupiter"},{"s":1891,"ri":243,"ph":"gods are at war with gods","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":40,"ref":"","n":"Proclus notes that Homer never sets the demiurgic monad or the gods abiding with Jupiter at war; only gods proceeding into multitude, near their subjects, are shown fighting.","q":"But it represents those Gods as warring against each other, who proceeding from the demiurgic monad, subside into multiform orders, become more partial, and more proximate to the objects of their government"},{"s":1893,"ri":245,"ph":"Hephaestus being flung out of heaven by his father","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":30,"ref":"","n":"Proclus takes the hurling of Vulcan to signify the progression of divinity from on high down to the last fabrication in sensibles, directed by the demiurgus and father.","q":"we must say, that the precipitation of Vulcan indicates the progression of a divine nature from on high, as far as to the last fabrication in sensibles, and this so as to be moved and perfected and directed by the demiurgus and father of all things."},{"s":1893,"ri":245,"ph":"the young person is unable to distinguish what is a deeper meaning","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":28,"ref":"","n":"Proclus takes this to show that Socrates blames Homer's fables only as unsuited to youth, while implying their hidden good needs a mystic, divinely inspired understanding.","q":"He likewise signifies that the recondite and occult good of fables requires a certain mystic and entheastic (i. e. divinely inspired) intelligence."},{"s":1893,"ri":245,"ph":"any battles of the gods that Homer has made up","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":38,"ref":"","n":"Proclus explains the battles of the gods as the fabled sign of real contrariety among divine genera, derived from the opposed first principles, bound and infinity.","q":"Is it therefore any longer wonderful, if the authors of fables, perceiving such contrariety in the Gods themselves and the first of beings, obscurely signified this to their pupils, through battles?"},{"s":1896,"ri":248,"ph":"since the god is actually good","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Additional Notes on the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-notes-1","p":90,"ref":"Book II","n":"Taylor, following Proclus, argues that the article in Plato's Greek ('the god is good') signifies every god, not the first god alone, since the article can mark either pre-eminence or a whole class.","q":"It is well observed by Proclus (in Plat. Polit. p. 355.), that when Plato says in this place, God is essentially good, he means every God"},{"s":1898,"ri":250,"ph":"two pitchers","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":51,"ref":"","n":"Proclus interprets Homer's two jars as the twofold primary causes of better and worse allotments, which the demiurgus Jupiter holds in himself and distributes to souls according to justice.","q":"These two fountains therefore of a better and worse condition of things, by which the demiurgus conducts souls according to justice, the poet mythologizing denominates tubs"},{"s":1899,"ri":251,"ph":"One with good, the other with wretched","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":55,"ref":"","n":"Proclus argues that the 'evil' in Homer's jar is not real evil but the inferior coordination, such as losing a son, which hinders a practical life; so Homer and Plato agree.","q":"In these verses, it appears that Homer does not make divinity the cause of real evils, since he calls the loss of a son, and the being deprived of his attendance in old age, evils."},{"s":1900,"ri":252,"ph":"Pandarus and the violation of oaths and truces","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":60,"ref":"","n":"Proclus answers that the gods did not cause the perjury: Pandarus' own avarice and impiety made him fit for an avenging energy, so Minerva sought him out.","q":"Again then, it must be in the first place said that the Gods were not the causes of this confused and disorderly conduct to the Trojans, but that they through their own depravity rendered themselves worthy of an energy of this kind"},{"s":1900,"ri":252,"ph":"if anyone says that Athena and Zeus brought this about","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":59,"ref":"","n":"Proclus explains that Jupiter and Minerva only call latent depravity into act so that the Trojans become ripe for punishment, like a surgeon opening an ulcer for future health.","q":"not that the Gods render men who are to be punished impious and unjust, but as calling into energy those that are adapted to the perpetration of such-like actions, that by once energizing according to their inward habit, and producing into light the progeny of depraved actions with which they are pregnant, they may become worthy of punishment."},{"s":1900,"ri":252,"ph":"Athena and Zeus brought this about","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":63,"ref":"","n":"Proclus stresses that Minerva only tests whether Pandarus will give himself up to the act, since divinity does not destroy free will even in the wicked.","q":"In the second place, this also must be considered, that Minerva is not said to prepare Pandarus for the deed, but only to try if he gave himself up to this energy. For divinity does not destroy the freedom of the will, not even in such as are consummately wicked:"},{"s":1900,"ri":252,"ph":"strife and contention of gods was caused by Themis and Zeus","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":72,"ref":"","n":"Proclus reads Jupiter's summons through Themis as converting the gods to the demiurgic monad while sending them to providence over the world, so that they govern lower natures by justice.","q":"But as the progressions of the Gods are not divulsed from the demiurgic monad, Themis first converts them to this monad."},{"s":1900,"ri":252,"ph":"strife and contention of gods","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":76,"ref":"","n":"Proclus takes the strife judged by Paris to signify the choice of lives under their presiding gods; Paris chose the amatory life without prudence, pursuing only an image of beauty.","q":"Paris also is said to have been appointed a judge of Minerva, Juno and Venus; and that of three lives which were proposed to him, he chose the amatory life: and this not with prudence, but recurring to apparent beauty"},{"s":1907,"ri":259,"ph":"being as noble and excellent as it is possible to be, each of them","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Additional Notes on the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-notes-1","p":90,"ref":"Book II","n":"Taylor takes this conclusion, that every god is as excellent as possible, as proof that the earlier phrase 'the god is good' referred to every god and not to the first only.","q":"But that he does not means the first only, is evident from his concluding after this, that every God is as much as possible most beautiful and excellent."},{"s":1908,"ri":260,"ph":"Gods in the likeness of strangers","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":80,"ref":"","n":"Proclus observes that these lines are spoken by one of the suitors, so Homer's own opinion should not be judged from them, as Plato's is not from Callicles or Thrasymachus.","q":"But here also it is requisite to collect by reasoning the divine dianoëtic conceptions of Homer, though I am not ignorant that the above verses are ascribed to one of the suitors, and that on this account the poet is free from blame."},{"s":1908,"ri":260,"ph":"Assume all sorts of disguises, as they visit our cities","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":83,"ref":"","n":"Proclus explains that the gods do not change; what is simple in them appears various because each recipient's phantasy participates them according to its own measure.","q":"Whether, therefore, some of the Gods have appeared similar to guests, or have been seen in some other form, it is not proper to attribute the apparent mutation to them, but we should say that the phantasy is varied in the different recipients."},{"s":1908,"ri":260,"ph":"Nor speak falsely of Proteus","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":84,"ref":"","n":"Proclus makes Proteus an angelic intellect of Neptune's series containing all generated forms; partial souls mistake the shifting of their own thoughts for a change in him.","q":"Partial souls therefore beholding Proteus, who is an intellect possessing many powers and full of forms, whilst at different times they convert themselves to the different forms which he contains, fancy that the transition of their own intellections is a mutation of the intelligible objects."},{"s":1915,"ri":267,"ph":"Zeus sends that dream to Agamemnon","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":92,"ref":"","n":"Proclus reports the common answer: the dream commanded an attack with the whole army (πανσυδιη), and Agamemnon's misreading, not Jupiter, caused the deception.","q":"But Agamemnon, not understanding the mandate, neglected the greatest part of his army, and, engaging in battle without the aid of Achilles, was frustrated in his expectations through his unskilfulness in judging of divine visions."},{"s":1915,"ri":267,"ph":"Zeus sends that dream to Agamemnon","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":93,"ref":"","n":"Proclus gives Syrianus' solution: the deception came from divinity for the good of the deceived, since good is better than truth, and the Greeks deserved punishment for wronging Achilles.","q":"It is better therefore to say that the deception was from divinity for the good of the deceived. For good is better than truth."},{"s":1932,"ri":273,"ph":"I would rather follow the plough as thrall to another man","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":102,"ref":"","n":"Proclus explains Achilles' wish: as a man of practical virtue he desires the body, the instrument his active life requires, which he cannot use in the separate life of Hades.","q":"Is it therefore wonderful that Achilles, who possessed practical virtue, should desire a life in conjunction with body, and which was capable of being subservient to his actions?"},{"s":1933,"ri":274,"ph":"a soul and an image, but there is no real heart of life in it","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":104,"ref":"","n":"Proclus finds here Homer's separation of soul from image and of intellect from soul: Achilles believed soul and image were in Hades, but not intellect or prudence.","q":"Hence Achilles, on beholding Patroclus speaking concerning the burial of his body, was led to believe that the soul and its image were in Hades, but that intellect was not there, nor prudence, by which these are used."},{"s":1933,"ri":274,"ph":"ghastly and mouldering, so the very gods shudder before them","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":107,"ref":"","n":"Proclus defends the epithet: the infernal region terrifies souls by its varied daemons and is most remote from the gods, as the extremity of the universe, full of material disorder.","q":"It is also most remote from the Gods, as being the extremity of the universe, and as possessing much of material disorder, and never enjoying the splendor of the solar rays."},{"s":1934,"ri":274,"ph":"leaving youth and manhood behind her","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":100,"ref":"","n":"Proclus explains Patroclus' lament: souls living a practical life cling to the bodily organ fitted to their actions and are indignant at being separated from it.","q":"For some live a more practic life, and, not yet deserting a life of this kind, embrace an organ adapted to practical energies, from which when they are separated they are indignant; as was the case with the soul of Patroclus,"},{"s":1935,"ri":275,"ph":"as when bats in the depth of an awful cave flitter","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":102,"ref":"","n":"Proclus says Homer likens to bats the souls that cling to the body, looking to the dark extremity of the universe and having the soul's winged nature grown gross and earthy.","q":"Such as these the divine poetry of Homer assimilates to bats, as looking to that which is dark in the universe, and its very extremity, and which may be denominated a stupendous cavern; and as having the winged nature of the soul gross and terrestrial."},{"s":1935,"ri":275,"ph":"so, gibbering, they went their way together","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":105,"ref":"","n":"Proclus takes the souls' shrill noise as the uncertain, material sound of shadowy vehicles weighed down by earthy vapours, the opposite of the harmonious sound of ascending souls.","q":"And such souls after deserting their bodies use shadowy vehicles, which are disturbed by the ponderous and terrene vapours of the Sirens, and utter an uncertain voice, and a material sound, which the Homeric poetry denominates a crashing noise."},{"s":1936,"ri":276,"ph":"names like ‘Cocytus’ and ‘Styx’","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":105,"ref":"","n":"Proclus holds that the rivers of Hades are not mere fables: they are underground places of punishment and purification containing outflows of the elements, hence called rivers.","q":"These places, as they contain the various defluxions of the elements on the earth, are called by poets rivers and streams."},{"s":1936,"ri":276,"ph":"Perhaps they are good for some other purpose","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":98,"ref":"","n":"Proclus notes that in rejecting such tales Socrates adds what holds of all fables, that they serve some other end, his fear being only that the guardians may come to dread death.","q":"For these Socrates reprobates, but at the same time adds, what is common to all fables, “that they contribute to something else; but we (says he) are afraid for our guardians, lest from these terrible relations, they should think death to be dreadful.”"},{"s":1939,"ri":279,"ph":"Lying now on his side, then again on his back, then face down","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":114,"ref":"","n":"Proclus defends Achilles' and Priam's grief: heroes engaged in war and practical life, not philosophers, fittingly feel and show passions over particular losses.","q":"Priam, therefore, and Achilles, neither being philosophers, nor willing to separate themselves from generation, nor living after the manner of the guardians of Plato’s republic,—if they lament and commiserate their familiars, it is by no means wonderful."},{"s":1942,"ri":282,"ph":"Ah me, my sorrow, the bitterness in this best of child-bearing","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":117,"ref":"","n":"Proclus explains divine weeping as the fabulists' sign of the gods' providence over mortal and perishable things, which naturally calls for tears; Thetis grieves without passion.","q":"Thetis therefore, and Jupiter, are said to lament those most dear to them, when in extreme danger—not that they are passively disposed after the manner of men, but because a certain separate providence proceeds from them, and gifts to particulars."},{"s":1942,"ri":282,"ph":"my heart is mourning for Hector","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":117,"ref":"","n":"Proclus says Jupiter's lament signifies a particular providence over an individual that the order of the universe opposes, so its object perishes; grief marks that providence, not passion.","q":"so that grief with them is a sign of the energy of a particular providence about individuals."},{"s":1945,"ri":285,"ph":"uncontrollable laughter","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":126,"ref":"","n":"Proclus defines the laughter of the gods as their exuberant energy in the universe and cause of its gladness; it is called unextinguished because their providence never fails.","q":"In short, we must define the laughter of the Gods to be their exuberant energy in the universe, and the cause of the gladness of all mundane natures."},{"s":1945,"ri":285,"ph":"as they saw Hephaistus bustling about the palace","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":126,"ref":"","n":"Proclus explains that Vulcan is the maker of the apparent world, and the gods' laughter at his motion shows them as fellow artificers perfecting his art and giving joy to the universe.","q":"And when the poet says that the Gods, being delighted with the motion of Vulcan, laughed with inextinguishable laughter, nothing else is indicated than that they are cooperating artificers; that they jointly give perfection to the art of Vulcan, and supernally impart joy to the universe."},{"s":1949,"ri":289,"ph":"You wine sack, with a dog’s eyes, with a deer’s heart","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":134,"ref":"","n":"Proclus defends Achilles' abuse of Agamemnon: Agamemnon did not excel in virtue but was benefited by others, and virtue, not command of numbers, deserves honour.","q":"For virtue is every where honourable, but not the instruments of virtue."},{"s":1950,"ri":290,"ph":"the tables are loaded with bread and meats","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":138,"ref":"","n":"Proclus notes that some read Ulysses' praise of the Phaeacian feast symbolically, and that literally he praises worthy joy, divine music and sufficiency, not pleasure.","q":"For with them the festival, the dainties, and the enchanting strain, have a different signification from that which is obvious to the multitude."},{"s":1951,"ri":291,"ph":"Zeus, awake, alone, while the other gods and humans too are sleeping","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":146,"ref":"","n":"Following Syrianus, Proclus reads Zeus' waking as his providence over the world and his sleep as a life separate from all subordinate natures, two energies he holds at once.","q":"wakefulness manifesting the providence of the Gods about the world, but sleep a life separate from all subordinate natures; though the Gods at the same time both providentially energize about the universe, and are established in themselves."},{"s":1951,"ri":291,"ph":"forgetting all the plans he had made","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":146,"ref":"","n":"Proclus denies real forgetfulness: when asleep with Juno in separate union, Jupiter still possesses and exercises his providential energy, containing both lives together.","q":"but that when asleep, and led together with Juno to a separate union, he is not forgetful of the other energy, but, possessing and energizing according to it, at the same time contains both."},{"s":1951,"ri":291,"ph":"he could not even wait to get into their chamber","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":146,"ref":"","n":"Proclus takes the union on the ground of Ida as supermundane, Ida meaning the region of ideas, while the chamber built by Vulcan signifies the sensible universe.","q":"The fable, therefore, does not diminish the dignity of the mighty Jupiter, by representing him as having connexion on the ground with Juno, and refusing to enter into her bed-chamber; for by this it insinuates that the connexion was supermundane, and not mundane."},{"s":1951,"ri":291,"ph":"not even when they first consorted together","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":152,"ref":"","n":"Proclus distinguishes two unions: the first, hidden from their parents, is the union proper to the pair; the greater one comes down to them from more perfect causes.","q":"According to the former of these, therefore, they are said to be concealed from their parents, in consequence of being allotted this union as peculiar to themselves; but according to the other they are levated to their causes"},{"s":1952,"ri":292,"ph":"Ares and Aphrodite being tied up by Hephaestus","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":156,"ref":"","n":"Proclus reads the adultery as the necessary union of dissimilar causes, Mars' division with Venus' harmony, and Vulcan's bonds as the demiurgic reasons binding generated natures.","q":"Fables therefore denominate this conspiring union of dissimilar causes adultery. But a communion of this kind is necessary to the universe, that contraries may be co-harmonized, and the mundane war terminate in peace."},{"s":1954,"ri":294,"ph":"Phoenix, as setting the standard when he advised him to accept gifts","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":158,"ref":"","n":"Proclus replies that Phoenix and Achilles treated the gifts as proof of the giver's repentance, not to satisfy avarice, and did not demand them but received them when offered.","q":"To these objections we shall briefly say, that Phœnix advises Achilles to lay aside his anger on receiving the gifts, and Achilles, on receiving them, did lay it aside, both of them considering the gifts as an argument of the repentance of the giver"},{"s":1955,"ri":295,"ph":"release a dead body if he was paid","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":158,"ref":"","n":"Proclus suggests that taking ransom for enemy bodies was then customary, and that depriving the enemy of wealth belongs to the art of command.","q":"But if Achilles restored the dead body of Hector to Priam, on its being redeemed by money, perhaps we may say that it was at that time customary to receive a ransom for the bodies of enemies."},{"s":1955,"ri":295,"ph":"most malignant of all gods","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":170,"ref":"","n":"Proclus argues that Achilles addresses not the god but a low daemoniacal Apollo, Hector's guardian, called most malignant because he kept Achilles' chief enemy unharmed.","q":"It must be considered, therefore, that these words were not addressed to a God, but to a dæmoniacal Apollo, and this not the first in rank, and coordinated with those that have a total dominion, but one who proximately presides over individuals"},{"s":1956,"ri":296,"ph":"disrespectful of the river, a god, and was prepared to do battle against him","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":177,"ref":"","n":"Proclus replies that Achilles fought not the god Xanthus but either the visible water blocking him or a local power, while Minerva and Neptune assisted him.","q":"For he was not disobedient to the God himself, but he either contended with the apparent water which hindered his impulse against the enemy, or with some one of the indigenous powers, the associate of the Gods in battle"},{"s":1956,"ri":296,"ph":"his own locks were already promised to the other river, Spercheius","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":180,"ref":"","n":"Proclus adds that the hair was not yet sacred to the river, since the vow was to be fulfilled on Achilles' return home, and that return was now denied him.","q":"I omit to say that the hairs of Achilles were not yet sacred to the river: for he who had promised to consecrate them on his return, when deprived of this, was also deprived of the consecration of his hairs."},{"s":1956,"ri":296,"ph":"the dragging of Hector’s body around the tomb of Patroclus","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":182,"ref":"","n":"Proclus defends the dragging as an ancient Thessalian custom, attested by the Cyrenaean poet, of dragging the slayer's body round the tomb of the slain.","q":"In the first place, then, it is said by the antients that this was the custom of the Thessalians, as the Cyrenæan poet also testifies"},{"s":1956,"ri":296,"ph":"the slaughter of the prisoners of war over his funeral pyre","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":190,"ref":"","n":"Following Syrianus, Proclus reads the rites at the pyre as imitating the theurgists' immortalising of the soul, concerned with Patroclus' separate soul and not merely his visible body.","q":"we must say that the whole transaction of Achilles concerning the pyre imitates the immortalizing of the soul (της ψυχης αποθανατισμος) by theurgists, and pertains to the separate soul of Patroclus."},{"s":1958,"ri":298,"ph":"Theseus, the son of Poseidon, and Perithous","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":194,"ref":"","n":"Proclus suggests that the two, as lovers of invisible and visible beauty, are said to carry off Helen and descend to Hades; Pirithous returned through intellect, Theseus remained.","q":"these heroes, being lovers both of unapparent and visible beauty, are fabled to have ravished Helen, and to have descended into the invisible regions"},{"s":1972,"ri":312,"ph":"the kind that employs both is found in epic poetry","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":12,"ref":"","n":"Proclus, as Taylor reports, recalls Plato's three forms of diction and assigns the Republic itself to the mixed form, which both narrates transactions and imitates the speakers.","q":"Such being the division of the forms of diction according to Plato, it is necessary to refer the present treatise to the mixed form of diction, which relates some things as transactions, and others as discourses"},{"s":1990,"ri":330,"ph":"We would anoint his head with myrrh, and give him a garland of wool","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Additional Notes on the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-notes-1","p":94,"ref":"Book III","n":"Taylor quotes the Greek scholium that this is a proverb for those dismissed from business: men poured oil on their heads at festivals when at leisure, and freed slaves wore wool round their heads.","q":"This is a proverb applied to those who are dismissed from business. For in festivals men poured oil on their head, as then resting from their usual avocations, and being alone at leisure for delight."},{"s":1990,"ri":330,"ph":"We would anoint his head with myrrh, and give him a garland of wool","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Additional Notes on the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-notes-1","p":94,"ref":"Book III","n":"Taylor gives the scholium's alternative explanation, citing Proclus: by a certain sacred law it was lawful to anoint the statues in the holiest temples with oil and crown them with wool.","q":"It was lawful to pour oil on the statues in the most holy temples, and to crown them with wool, and this according to a certain sacred law, as the great Proclus says."},{"s":2075,"ri":415,"ph":"he mixed in gold","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Additional Notes on the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-notes-1","p":96,"ref":"Book III","n":"Taylor sees an allusion to Hesiod's ages of mankind, the golden age signifying the intellectual life, pure and impassive, of which gold, never rusting, is an image.","q":"Among these, the golden age indicates an intellectual life. For such a life is pure, impassive, and free from sorrow; and of this impassivity and purity gold is an image, from never being subject to rust or putrefaction."},{"s":2075,"ri":415,"ph":"In the case of the auxiliaries, he mixed in silver","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Additional Notes on the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-notes-1","p":96,"ref":"Book III","n":"Taylor reads the silver of Hesiod's ages as a rustic, natural life devoted to the care of the body without reaching extreme vice, and the bronze as a cruel, tyrannic, wholly passive life.","q":"By the silver age a rustic and natural life is implied, in which the attention of the rational soul is entirely directed to the care of the body, but without proceeding to the extremity of vice."},{"s":2122,"ri":451,"ph":"the manners of music do not change without the most important civic laws changing too","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"On the Republic (extract, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-1","p":105,"ref":"Book IV","n":"Proclus uses this saying as evidence that Socrates chooses as guardians those most naturally adapted to music, since the polity is dissolved by neglect of music, not of gymnastic.","q":"Of the truth of which I consider this to be an argument, that the whole polity, as Socrates says, is dissolved, not through a neglect of gymnastic, but of music."},{"s":2160,"ri":489,"ph":"it makes them sing the same song","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"On the Republic (extract, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-1","p":102,"ref":"Book IV","n":"Proclus explains why Socrates calls temperance a harmony like the octave: it alone pervades all the parts of the soul, joining the higher motions with the lower and producing one life from many.","q":"it will be adapted to the harmony of the soul, pervading through all the parts, conjoining the superior with the inferior motions, connascently harmonizing the intensions of the one with the remissions of the other, and truly producing one life from many."},{"s":2179,"ri":508,"ph":"as the Thracians and Scythians do","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"On the Republic (extract, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-1","p":107,"ref":"Book IV","n":"Proclus explains the national characters as arising from the soul: Greeks are more apt for wisdom, Thracians more irascible, Phoenicians more mercenary, according to which part of the soul rules in each nation.","q":"And the Greeks indeed have a greater aptitude to wisdom (when we speak of the whole nation), the Thracians are more irascible, and the Phœnicians more mercenary than either of these. But this arises from the soul, through which in some nations reason has dominion, in others anger, and in others desire."},{"s":2182,"ri":511,"ph":"the same thing will not, at the same time, do or suffer opposites","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"On the Republic (extract, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-1","p":108,"ref":"Book IV","n":"Proclus takes this principle as Socrates' universally acknowledged premise for distinguishing the soul's parts, illustrating how one thing can do or suffer contraries in different parts or with reference to different things.","q":"previously assuming as a thing universally acknowledged, that it is impossible for the same thing, according to the same, and with reference to the same, to do or suffer contraries; but that this may be accomplished by the same thing in a different respect."},{"s":2197,"ri":526,"ph":"people who refuse to drink although they are thirsty","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"On the Republic (extract, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-1","p":109,"ref":"Book IV","n":"Proclus illustrates this with the fevered patient, in whom reason forbids drinking while desire urges it, showing that reason and desire are distinct parts of the soul.","q":"But, prior to these, this opposition is seen in the diseased, reason admonishing them not to drink if the body is hot with a fever, but desire calling on them to drink, and the two parts thus opposing each other."},{"s":2205,"ri":534,"ph":"a natural auxiliary to the rational element","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"On the Republic (extract, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-1","p":103,"ref":"Book IV","n":"Proclus argues that anger sides with reason against desire not from any kinship with reason but because of desire's obstinacy; in essence anger is closer to desire than to reason.","q":"But it does this through the pertinacity of desire, through which it often contumaciously excites reason against desire, and not through any alliance with reason."},{"s":2205,"ri":534,"ph":"the commercial, the auxiliary and the deliberative","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":10,"ref":"","n":"Proclus, as Taylor reports, maps the city's three classes onto the soul: the guardians who deliberate answer to reason, the auxiliaries to anger, the mercenary class to desire.","q":"the guardian , as that which consults, to reason; the auxiliary , as engaging in war, to anger; and the mercenary , as supplying the wants of nature, to the desiderative part of the soul."},{"s":2206,"ri":535,"ph":"He smote his breast and rebuked his heart","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"On the Republic (extract, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-1","p":109,"ref":"Book IV","n":"Proclus takes the Homeric example of Ulysses as the case of one who is injured but restrains the anger urging revenge, proving that reason and anger are not the same.","q":"Prior to both these, however, it is seen in those that are injured, but do not revenge the injury, though they are incited to vengeance by anger, as in the instance which Homer presents us with in Ulysses"},{"s":2209,"ri":538,"ph":"the spirited part should be subordinate, and fight by its side","c":"Plutarch","e":"early-empire","y":100,"w":"Platonic Questions","k":"plut-moralia-4","p":1693,"ref":"Question IX","n":"Plutarch takes spirit's intermediate role as grounds for its middle place: reason only governs, while spirit both obeys reason and commands appetite when appetite disobeys.","q":"For it is the nature of reason to govern, and of the irascible faculty both to govern and be governed, since it is obsequious to reason, and commands the appetitive faculty when this is disobedient to reason."},{"s":2219,"ri":548,"ph":"the three defining notes of the musical scale, the highest, lowest and middle","c":"Plutarch","e":"early-empire","y":100,"w":"Platonic Questions","k":"plut-moralia-4","p":1692,"ref":"Question IX","n":"Plutarch asks whether Plato puts reason or spirit in the middle, and argues that by natural order spirit is the middle and reason the highest, called hypate.","q":"Indeed, according to the natural system of the parts, the place of the irascible faculty must be in the middle, and of the rational in the highest, which the Greeks call hypate."},{"s":2219,"ri":548,"ph":"the three defining notes of the musical scale, the highest, lowest and middle","c":"Plutarch","e":"early-empire","y":100,"w":"Platonic Questions","k":"plut-moralia-4","p":1695,"ref":"Question IX","n":"Plutarch notes that with spirit in the middle the concords are preserved: spirit to reason a fourth, spirit to appetite a fifth, and reason to appetite an octave.","q":"This order also keeps the analogy of the symphonies, i.e. the proportion of the irascible to the rational (which is placed as hypate) making the diatessaron (or fourth), that of the irascible to the concupiscent (or nete) making the diapente (or fifth), and that of the rational to the concupiscent (as hypate to nete) making an octave or diapason."},{"s":2219,"ri":548,"ph":"harmonising the three elements","c":"Plutarch","e":"early-empire","y":100,"w":"Platonic Questions","k":"plut-moralia-4","p":1698,"ref":"Question IX","n":"Plutarch alternatively argues that the parts should be ranked by power, not place, so that reason acts as mese, tightening and slackening the other two and so making the harmony.","q":"But it holds the chief and highest power, as mese to hypate, in respect of the concupiscent; as mese to nete, in respect of the irascible; insomuch as it depresses and heightens, — and in fine makes a harmony, — by abating what is too much and by not suffering them to flatten and grow dull."},{"s":2219,"ri":548,"ph":"the highest, lowest and middle","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"On the Republic (extract, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-1","p":102,"ref":"Book IV","n":"Proclus maps the three notes onto reason, anger and desire: anger, the middle, forms with reason the fifth (diapente) and with desire the fourth (diatessaron), the whole making the octave.","q":"But of these, anger being the middle, in one part produces the symphony called diatessaron, and in another that which is denominated diapente; the symphony of reason to anger forming the diapente, and that of anger to desire the diatessaron."},{"s":2219,"ri":548,"ph":"and any others that are in between","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"On the Republic (extract, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-1","p":118,"ref":"Book IV","n":"Proclus answers the worry that Socrates admits further parts of the soul: the things in between are mixed lives, such as the learned yet ambitious, not additional parts.","q":"These then are the forms of life between reason and anger, and anger and desire; and are not other parts of the soul, but become, from the mixture of these, various instead of simple."},{"s":2298,"ri":612,"ph":"our rulers will have to resort to falsehood on a large scale, and deception too","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":93,"ref":"","n":"Proclus cites Socrates' own use of falsehood for the guardians' city as precedent: divinity may benefit some through truth and others through falsehood.","q":"Whence also Socrates himself, when he is framing laws for the guardians of his republic, orders falsehood to be employed, through the opinion of the stupid, who are not otherwise able to obtain the good which is adapted to their condition."},{"s":2397,"ri":711,"ph":"the children’s riddle about the eunuch striking the bat","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Additional Notes on the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-notes-1","p":125,"ref":"Book V","n":"Taylor, following the Greek scholiast, identifies this as the riddle of Clearchus and gives its wording: a man and not a man, seeing and not seeing, struck and did not strike a bird and not a bird.","q":"This, according to the Greek Scholiast on this part, was the riddle of Clearchus, and is as follows: A man and not a man, seeing and yet not seeing, struck and yet did not strike, a bird and not a bird, sitting and not sitting, on a tree and yet not on a tree."},{"s":2397,"ri":711,"ph":"what it was struck by and what it was sitting upon","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Additional Notes on the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-notes-1","p":125,"ref":"Book V","n":"Taylor gives the riddle's solution: a eunuch blind in one eye struck with a pumice stone the wing of a bat perched on a reed.","q":"That is, a Eunuch blind of one eye struck with a pumice stone the wing of a bat perched on a reed."},{"s":2549,"ri":844,"ph":"a line which has been divided into two unequal sections","c":"Plutarch","e":"early-empire","y":100,"w":"Platonic Questions","k":"plut-moralia-4","p":1650,"ref":"Question III","n":"Plutarch asks what Plato means by dividing the line unequally, and which section, the intelligible or the sensible, is the greater, since Plato does not say.","q":"But what Does he Mean by Dividing the Universe into Unequal Parts? And which of the Sections, the Intelligible or the Sensible, is the Greater?"},{"s":2549,"ri":844,"ph":"a line which has been divided into two unequal sections","c":"Plutarch","e":"early-empire","y":100,"w":"Platonic Questions","k":"plut-moralia-4","p":1651,"ref":"Question III","n":"Plutarch first argues the sensible section is greater: the intelligible is indivisible and contracted into little, while the sensible is divisible, spread through bodies, and multiplied in many images of each original.","q":"At first glance it will appear that the sensible is the greater portion. For the essence of intelligibles being indivisible, and in the same respect ever the same, is contracted into a little, and pure; but an essence divisible and running through bodies constitutes the sensible part."},{"s":2549,"ri":844,"ph":"a line which has been divided into two unequal sections","c":"Plutarch","e":"early-empire","y":100,"w":"Platonic Questions","k":"plut-moralia-4","p":1652,"ref":"Question III","n":"Plutarch then gives the opposing case for the intelligible being greater: it contains the sensible, as the world-soul encloses the body of the universe, and the container is always greater than the contained.","q":"Besides, the thing contained is ever less than the containing, and the nature of the universe contains the sensible in the intelligible."},{"s":2558,"ri":853,"ph":"the faculty of geometers and the like not intelligence, but understanding","c":"Plutarch","e":"early-empire","y":100,"w":"Platonic Questions","k":"plut-moralia-4","p":1651,"ref":"Question III","n":"Plutarch explains understanding as reason operating in mathematics, where intelligibles are seen as in mirrors, while reason alone judges the intelligibles themselves.","q":"Now the reason is the only criterion of intelligibles; and the understanding is the reason in the mathematics, where intelligibles appear as if in mirrors."},{"s":2563,"ri":854,"ph":"assign belief to the third, imagination to the last","c":"Plutarch","e":"early-empire","y":100,"w":"Platonic Questions","k":"plut-moralia-4","p":1649,"ref":"Question III","n":"Plutarch sets out the four criteria Plato assigns to the four sections: reason to the forms, understanding to mathematics, belief to sensible bodies, and conjecture to images and likenesses.","q":"Moreover, to every one of these four he has assigned its proper criterion; — to the first reason; to the mathematics, the understanding; to sensibles, belief; to images and likenesses, conjecture."},{"s":2724,"ri":1000,"ph":"come into existence from oak or from rock","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"On the Republic (extract, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-1","p":107,"ref":"Book IV","n":"Proclus glosses the oak as an image of the lowest, vegetative nature and the rock as inanimate solid body: the differences among men and nations come from neither, but from the soul.","q":"for it is not, says he, either from an oak, or a rock, viz. it is not from the lowest nature, of which an oak is the image, nor from an inanimate and solid body, so far as body, for this is indicated by a rock."},{"s":2942,"ri":1212,"ph":"many-headed beast","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"On the Republic (extract, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-1","p":113,"ref":"Book IV","n":"Proclus explains that desire is called a many-headed beast because the irrational life, being close to the manifold body, is both one and many; hence love of wealth and love of pleasure are not separate parts.","q":"In thus doubting, however, we forget that desire is called by Socrates a many-headed beast, because the irrational life is both one and many, as being proximate to the body, which is entirely manifold and divisible"},{"s":2942,"ri":1212,"ph":"many-headed beast","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"On the Republic (extract, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-1","p":113,"ref":"Book IV","n":"Proclus stresses that Plato calls desire one animal with many heads, not many animals: it lives by different heads at different times but is always a lover of the body.","q":"Hence Plato does not say that it is many animals, but calls it one animal having many heads, and living at different times according to its different heads, but being always a lover of body."},{"s":3086,"ri":1342,"ph":"perched on top of each of the circles was a Siren","c":"Plutarch","e":"early-empire","y":100,"w":"On the Generation of the Soul in the Timaeus","k":"plut-moralia-4","p":1795,"ref":"","n":"Plutarch contrasts the moderns, who add an extra note below hypate, with Plato, who makes his addition at the top: each of the eight spheres bears a Siren, and together they sound one harmony.","q":"Whereas Plato makes his addition in the upper part; for in his Republic he says, that every one of the eight spheres rolls about a Siren which is fixed upon each of the tuneful globes"},{"s":3096,"ri":1352,"ph":"chose the most extreme tyranny. Out of stupidity","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":65,"ref":"","n":"Proclus compares Pandarus with the soul that first chose tyranny in the myth of Er: the choice came from the chooser's stupidity, not from the prophet who offered it.","q":"For neither is the prophet in Plato the cause of a tyrannic life, but the intemperance of him that chose it."}]});
